<Project>
  <!--
    Stimpak (the native StarCraft II/SC:R/WC3:R Battle.net chat client, ncarrillo/superiority)
    is consumed as a NuGet package as of 2026-08-24, replacing the git submodule + local Rust
    build this project used before — the package now ships prebuilt native binaries for every
    platform Invigoration targets (win-x64, linux-x64, osx-arm64, osx-x64), so building from
    source no longer needs a Rust toolchain at all.

    Getting this to actually load correctly at runtime took three attempts, empirically tested
    (a scratch console project, three build shapes: `dotnet run`, self-contained loose-files
    publish, self-contained single-file publish) rather than assumed — worth recording why the
    final shape looks the way it does:

    1. The package's own build (imported automatically as "buildTransitive") copies its native
       library flatly into the output root, where it collides — on a case-insensitive
       filesystem (Windows) — with its own managed Stimpak.dll: same name apart from casing, so
       only one survives the copy, and it isn't the managed one. Every P/Invoke then fails with
       FileNotFoundException trying to load the (now-missing) managed assembly.
       Fix: ExcludeAssets="buildTransitive" turns off the package's own copy entirely.
    2. That alone isn't enough for a *published* build: leaving the SDK's own standard native-
       asset resolution enabled additionally throws NETSDK1152 ("found multiple publish output
       files with the same relative path") during `dotnet publish` specifically — self-contained
       publish intentionally flattens native assets that a plain `dotnet build` would otherwise
       leave nested under runtimes/<rid>/native/, and that flattening is what re-collides it with
       the managed assembly, this time as a hard build error instead of a silent runtime one.
       Fix: ExcludeAssets="native" too, then stage the file ourselves — nested under
       runtimes/<rid>/native/, never flat — via our own Copy targets that run strictly after
       Build/Publish (earlier hooks like ResolvedFileToPublish or a plain None item were tried
       and didn't reliably survive single-file publish's own file-list computation).
    3. Even placed correctly and non-collidingly on disk, .NET's *default* native-library
       resolver still checks the flat app directory first — where the managed Stimpak.dll still
       sits — before it ever probes runtimes/<rid>/native/, so a bare P/Invoke call still finds
       the wrong file and fails with EntryPointNotFoundException (a different exception than
       problem 1's FileNotFoundException, but the same root cause: OS-level LoadLibrary doesn't
       care that "stimpak" and "Stimpak" are different files to us).
       Fix: bypass OS search order entirely with NativeLibrary.SetDllImportResolver, registered
       once at app startup — see Invigoration.Core's StimpakNativeResolver.cs. That's real
       application code (not something an MSBuild props file can do), so importing this file
       alone isn't sufficient on its own; StimpakNativeResolver.Register() also needs to run
       before the first StimpakClient is constructed.

    All three fixes were verified together, not just individually, against all three build
    shapes above before being folded back into this project.

    Import this from any project whose own output needs to actually P/Invoke into Stimpak at
    runtime — not just Invigoration.App, but also Invigoration.Core (which calls
    StimpakNativeResolver.Register() — see BotEngine.Sc2.cs) and Invigoration.Core.Tests, since
    some of its tests construct a real StimpakClient (see BotEngineSc2ChannelStateTests.cs).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Stimpak" Version="0.1.0-preview.1" ExcludeAssets="buildTransitive;native" GeneratePathProperty="true" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- $(RuntimeIdentifier) is only set for a RID-specific build (e.g. `dotnet publish -r
         win-x64`); an ordinary `dotnet build`/`dotnet test` has no RID at all, so fall back to
         whatever RID the current machine's SDK reports for itself. -->
    <_StimpakRid Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">$(RuntimeIdentifier)</_StimpakRid>
    <_StimpakRid Condition="'$(_StimpakRid)' == ''">$(NETCoreSdkRuntimeIdentifier)</_StimpakRid>
    <_StimpakNativeFileName Condition="'$(_StimpakRid)' == 'win-x64'">stimpak.dll</_StimpakNativeFileName>
    <_StimpakNativeFileName Condition="'$(_StimpakRid)' == 'linux-x64'">libstimpak.so</_StimpakNativeFileName>
    <_StimpakNativeFileName Condition="'$(_StimpakRid)' == 'osx-arm64' or '$(_StimpakRid)' == 'osx-x64'">libstimpak.dylib</_StimpakNativeFileName>
    <_StimpakNativeSource>$(PkgStimpak)\runtimes\$(_StimpakRid)\native\$(_StimpakNativeFileName)</_StimpakNativeSource>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="CopyStimpakNativeAfterBuild" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(_StimpakNativeFileName)' != '' and Exists('$(_StimpakNativeSource)')">
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(_StimpakNativeSource)" DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)runtimes\$(_StimpakRid)\native\" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="CopyStimpakNativeAfterPublish" AfterTargets="Publish"
          Condition="'$(_StimpakNativeFileName)' != '' and Exists('$(_StimpakNativeSource)')">
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(_StimpakNativeSource)" DestinationFolder="$(PublishDir)runtimes\$(_StimpakRid)\native\" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>
</Project>
